Patch summary:
An application that goes away without destroying its Vulkan objects no
longer leaves their handles in the boxed handle table. When a device or an
instance is torn down, its remaining buffers, memories, command buffers,
command pools, descriptor pools, shader modules, pipelines, pipeline caches,
pipeline layouts, framebuffers and render passes were destroyed in the
driver, but their boxed handles stayed until the renderer exited; the records
of those types that had no boxed handle now keep it. Destroying a command
pool passed its command buffers' driver handles to delete_VkCommandBuffer,
which takes boxed ones, so those handles and their read streams stayed as
well: about 850 after each run of 3DMark Wild Life Extreme. A handle removed
after a delay (most destroyed objects) now leaves the map from driver handles
to boxed ones too, which it never did.
